---
/**
 * editorial.astro — Editorial dialect demo
 *
 * Demonstrates the prose-forward register: ~960px reading column, drop-cap
 * on the first paragraph, .bleed available for figures or tables that want
 * the wider 1280px track. Use this layout for essays, working papers, and
 * long-form writing.
 *
 * The drop-cap fires automatically on the first <p> inside .prose. Headings
 * (<h2>, <h3>) get baseline-rhythm spacing from typography.css.
 */
import EditorialDialect from '../layouts/EditorialDialect.astro';
import Lockup from '../components/Lockup.astro';
---

<EditorialDialect
  title="Editorial"
  description="Editorial dialect demo — prose-forward layout with drop-cap and a wider bleed track for figures."
  ogType="article"
>
  <header class="ed-header">
    <Lockup variant="editorial" height={32} eager />
  </header>

  <h1>A title for the essay, balanced and unhurried.</h1>

  <p>
    This is the first paragraph of the essay. The first letter renders as a
    drop-cap because the dialect targets <code>.prose &gt; p:first-of-type</code>
    in its scoped style block. Everything after that follows the baseline
    rhythm defined in <code>typography.css</code>: 16px body, 1.6 line-height,
    66ch reading measure. Replace this body copy with your own — the layout
    will hold up to anything from a 600-word post to a 5,000-word paper.
  </p>

  <p>
    Subsequent paragraphs do not get a drop-cap. They just sit in the prose
    column and obey the measure. The point of this register is that the
    reader is reading <em>linearly</em>, not scanning, so the column width,
    the line length, and the typeface all conspire to keep them in flow.
  </p>

  <h2>A second-level heading</h2>

  <p>
    Headings sit comfortably above the next paragraph without ornament. If
    you need to surface a figure or a wide table, wrap it in a <code>.bleed</code>
    container — it will escape the reading column out to the wider editorial
    track without the reader losing their place.
  </p>

  <p>
    Footnotes, citations, and metadata can sit in the quieter <code>.meta</code>
    register defined in the dialect's scoped style block, which uses the
    smaller font size and the steel color for subordinate information.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>

  <p>
    Replace this address with whatever channel makes sense for editorial
    correspondence: <a href="mailto:editorial@{{PROJECT_DOMAIN}}">editorial@{{PROJECT_DOMAIN}}</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="meta">
    Last updated: replace this with a real date. Editorial pages benefit
    from a visible "last touched" line, especially if they are evergreen.
  </p>
</EditorialDialect>

<style>
  .ed-header {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  /* Keep code spans monospaced inside the prose column; the global rule
     leaves them untouched so the dialect can opt in here. */
  :global(.dialect--editorial .prose code) {
    font-family: var(--ff-mono);
    font-size: 0.92em;
    background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--steel) 8%, transparent);
    padding: 0.05em 0.3em;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }
</style>
